"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical pathology report indicates a Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1) and pT2c stage with no extraprostatic extension, but positive surgical margins. The absence of lymph nodes removed and the lack of seminal vesicle invasion are favorable findings. Countervailing to this, the positive margins introduce a risk factor. Missing nodal sampling widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted as missing, which limits the assessment of tumor extent. The preoperative PSA was 4.\n\nThe primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA and its trajectory, as previous notes are absent. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent changes would significantly revise this prognosis. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 49 months without an observed event. Missing nodal sampling also contributes to the overall uncertainty in the outcome prediction. The fixed estimated horizon is 49.00 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 29.40–68.60 months; its coverage has not been validated."
